  "body": "Some elements (like `<center>`) are not part of HTML, but are still widely used on major websites (such as Google) and are thus *de facto* standardized (in that any browser is in practice required to support them).  In Google’s case, this is because it is faster than using CSS for that purpose.\r\n\r\nFurthermore, it is much easier to sanitize HTML than HTML+CSS, so most websites that filter user-submitted HTML do not allow CSS.  Therefore, removing tags like `<center>` is a loss of functionality for these websites – `<center>` IIRC is often on whitelists, since it is obviously harmless.\r\n\r\nSince major websites use obsolete HTML elements and/or attributes, and have no intention to change this, any user agent must support them.  Therefore, it is best to place them back in the standard (perhaps with a tag indicating that they are deprecated) so that their behavior will at least be consistent cross-browser.",
